Here's the script. Paste the whole thing into **Extensions > Apps Script**, save, and run each function separately from the editor's Run menu (or add a quick UI menu if you prefer — the optional block at the bottom does that).

**Permissions it will request:** "See, edit, create, and delete your spreadsheets." That's the standard Sheets scope; it doesn't touch Drive, Gmail, or anything else.

---

```javascript
// ── CONFIG ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
const RAW_SHEET   = "Sheet1";   // name of your raw/clean data sheet
const PARK_COL    = 6;          // column F = PreferredPark (1-indexed)
const CLEAN_COL   = 10;         // column J = Clean park output (1-indexed)
const HEADER_ROW  = 1;          // row 1 is headers, data starts row 2
const PARKMAP_SHEET = "ParkMap";
// ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/** Step 1 — Build ParkMap with distinct keys and counts. */
function buildParkMap() {
  const ss      = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet();
  const rawSh   = ss.getSheetByName(RAW_SHEET);
  const lastRow = rawSh.getLastRow();

  // Pull raw park values (column F, skipping header)
  const parkValues = rawSh
    .getRange(HEADER_ROW + 1, PARK_COL, lastRow - HEADER_ROW, 1)
    .getValues()
    .flat();

  // Build key → count map
  const counts = {};
  for (const val of parkValues) {
    const key = normalize(val);
    if (key === "") continue;
    counts[key] = (counts[key] || 0) + 1;
  }

  // Write or clear the ParkMap sheet
  let pmSh = ss.getSheetByName(PARKMAP_SHEET);
  if (!pmSh) {
    pmSh = ss.insertSheet(PARKMAP_SHEET);
  } else {
    pmSh.clearContents();
  }

  const rows = [["key", "canonical", "count"]];
  for (const [key, count] of Object.entries(counts).sort()) {
    rows.push([key, "", count]);   // leave canonical blank for you to fill
  }
  pmSh.getRange(1, 1, rows.length, 3).setValues(rows);

  SpreadsheetApp.getUi().alert(
    `ParkMap built: ${rows.length - 1} distinct keys. Fill column B, then run applyParkMap.`
  );
}

/** Step 2 — Read filled ParkMap and write canonical names into Clean!J. */
function applyParkMap() {
  const ss    = SpreadsheetApp.getActiveSpreadsheet();
  const rawSh = ss.getSheetByName(RAW_SHEET);
  const pmSh  = ss.getSheetByName(PARKMAP_SHEET);

  if (!pmSh) {
    SpreadsheetApp.getUi().alert("Run buildParkMap first.");
    return;
  }

  // Load ParkMap into a lookup object (skip header row)
  const pmData = pmSh.getDataRange().getValues();
  const lookup = {};
  for (let i = 1; i < pmData.length; i++) {
    const key       = String(pmData[i][0]).trim();
    const canonical = String(pmData[i][1]).trim();
    if (key !== "" && canonical !== "") lookup[key] = canonical;
  }

  // Read raw park column, write cleaned values to CLEAN_COL
  const lastRow   = rawSh.getLastRow();
  const dataRows  = lastRow - HEADER_ROW;
  const parkRange = rawSh.getRange(HEADER_ROW + 1, PARK_COL, dataRows, 1);
  const parkVals  = parkRange.getValues();

  const output = parkVals.map(([val]) => {
    const key = normalize(val);
    if (key === "") return ["REVIEW"];
    return [lookup[key] ?? "REVIEW"];
  });

  rawSh.getRange(HEADER_ROW + 1, CLEAN_COL, dataRows, 1).setValues(output);

  const reviewed = output.filter(r => r[0] === "REVIEW").length;
  SpreadsheetApp.getUi().alert(
    `Done. ${output.length - reviewed} mapped, ${reviewed} marked REVIEW.`
  );
}

/** Normalize a raw park string to a lookup key. */
function normalize(val) {
  return String(val).toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z]/g, "").trim();
}

// ── OPTIONAL: adds a menu so you can run from the sheet, not just the editor ─
function onOpen() {
  SpreadsheetApp.getUi()
    .createMenu("🧹 Data Tools")
    .addItem("1. Build ParkMap",  "buildParkMap")
    .addItem("2. Apply ParkMap",  "applyParkMap")
    .addToUi();
}
```

---

**How to use it**

Paste into Apps Script and save. The first time you run either function (or when the sheet reloads if you added `onOpen`), Google will ask you to authorize — click through the standard OAuth prompt.

Run `buildParkMap` first. It scans column F, writes every distinct normalized key plus its occurrence count into `ParkMap!A:C`, and leaves column B blank for you to fill in canonical names (e.g. `westside → West Side`). Keys with higher counts are the ones worth doing first.

Once you've filled in column B for all the keys you recognize, run `applyParkMap`. It reads your mappings and writes results into column J, marking anything without a match as `REVIEW`. You can run it again any time after adding more canonical names to the map — it just overwrites column J.

The two config constants to double-check before running are `RAW_SHEET` (your sheet's tab name) and `CLEAN_COL` (column J = 10, but adjust if your layout differs).

You said two tweaks but listed three — no worries, here are all three:

